---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RatingPill from '../../components/RatingPill.astro';
import { ratingBlurb, ratingOrder } from '../../data/topics';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How to read the evidence"
  description="A short guide to study designs, bias, effect sizes and the four-step rating scale used on Sound Evidence."
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="container prose">
      <span class="eyebrow">Guide</span>
      <h1>How to read <span class="hero-italic">the evidence</span></h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Not all “studies show” statements are equal. Here’s a five-minute guide to what makes a finding
        trustworthy, and how we turn research into a rating.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" style="padding-top:0.5rem">
    <div class="container prose">
      <h2>1. The evidence ladder</h2>
      <p>From weakest to strongest, roughly:</p>
      <ol class="ladder">
        <li><strong>Testimonials and personal stories.</strong> Real experiences, but they can’t tell you what caused a change.</li>
        <li><strong>Theory, lab and animal studies.</strong> Useful for ideas (for example, 40 Hz research in mice), but results often don’t carry over to people.</li>
        <li><strong>Before-and-after studies with no control group.</strong> People often feel better after any calm hour of rest. Without a comparison group, we can’t tell what did the work.</li>
        <li><strong>Small randomized or crossover trials.</strong> Better: chance decides who gets what. But small samples swing easily, and “pilot” means “first look.”</li>
        <li><strong>Large, well-run randomized controlled trials (RCTs).</strong> The best single-study design for testing whether something works.</li>
        <li><strong>Systematic reviews and meta-analyses</strong> (Cochrane reviews are a well-known example). These gather all the trials on a question and judge their quality. One review is worth more than any single exciting study.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>2. Five questions to ask any study</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Compared with what?</strong> Silence, rest, ordinary music, or nothing at all? A sound bath compared with sitting in a waiting room isn’t a fair test of the sound.</li>
        <li><strong>Could people tell which group they were in?</strong> In music research, people almost always can, so expectation (the placebo effect) can account for part of the result. Reviews often rate these trials as having a <em>high risk of bias</em> for this reason. That doesn’t make them worthless; it makes them less certain.</li>
        <li><strong>How big was the effect?</strong> “Statistically significant” only means probably not chance. Effect sizes are often reported as <em>d</em>, <em>g</em> or SMD. By common convention about 0.2 is small, 0.5 medium and 0.8 large.</li>
        <li><strong>What was measured, and for how long?</strong> Feeling calmer right after a session is different from lower blood pressure a month later. Self-reported outcomes (how you feel) and objective ones (what a device measures) sometimes disagree, as in the sleep research.</li>
        <li><strong>Who delivered it?</strong> Music therapy by a credentialed therapist, recorded music on headphones, a singing-bowl session and a vibration bed are different interventions. Evidence for one doesn’t transfer to another.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>3. Certainty labels you’ll see in reviews</h2>
      <p>
        Cochrane and many other reviews use the GRADE system to rate how confident they are in a result:
        <strong>high</strong>, <strong>moderate</strong>, <strong>low</strong> or <strong>very low</strong>
        certainty. “Moderate” means the true effect is probably close to the estimate. “Low” or “very low” means
        the true effect may be quite different. When a review gives a certainty rating, we report it.
      </p>

      <h2>4. Our four-step rating</h2>
      <div class="legend legend-stack">
        {ratingOrder.map((r) => (
          <div class="legend-item">
            <RatingPill rating={r} />
            <p class="small">{ratingBlurb[r]}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p>
        A rating always applies to a <em>specific</em> claim and outcome, shown on each topic page as
        “Applies to.” For example, music for anxiety around surgery is rated Strong, but that doesn’t make
        432 Hz music, singing bowls or “sound healing” in general strong.
      </p>

      <h2>5. How we check sources</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Every citation was looked up in PubMed, Crossref or on the publisher or agency page, and its title, authors, journal, year and DOI were confirmed.</li>
        <li>Numbers on this site come only from the abstract or full text we actually read. Each source is labeled “Checked: abstract,” “full text” or “official page.”</li>
        <li>We don’t quote studies we couldn’t open, and we don’t round weak findings up.</li>
        <li>Spot an error or a newer review? Please tell us. Ratings will change as evidence does.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="disclaimer">Disclaimer</h2>
      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          <strong>Educational only, not medical advice.</strong> Sound Evidence summarizes published research for
          learning. Nothing here diagnoses, treats, cures or prevents any disease, and it isn’t a substitute for
          care from a qualified health professional. Don’t stop or change any treatment based on what you read here.
          If you have a health concern, talk with your doctor or another licensed clinician.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="btn-row">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/evidence/">Open the evidence table</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/for-practitioners/">For practitioners</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
